 ],
 "literal": "Hand bangles slipping and body pallor spreading / Sowing wild rice on the cloud-resting mountain slope / With the lord of the land who ripens it with waterfalls / I do not unite, O friend, yet that love is indeed great!",
 "translation": "My bangles slip from my hands and a pale tint spreads over my body. Yet, I do not unite with the lord of the land where wild rice, sown on cloud-capped mountain slopes, is nurtured and ripened by the waterfalls. O friend, this love of mine is indeed immense!",
 "modern": "தோழியே! மேகங்கள் தவழும் மலைச்சரிவில் மலைநெல்லை விதைத்து, அருவி நீரால் அதனை விளையச் செய்யும் நாட்டை உடைய தலைவனுடன் நான் கூடவில்லை. அதனால் என் கைகளின் வளையல்கள் கழலுகின்றன; உடம்பில் பசலை நிறம் பரவுகிறது. ஆயினும், அவன்பால் கொண்ட என் அன்பு மிகவும் பெரியது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend during a period of separation from the hero, who has delayed their marriage. She describes the physical toll of her pining, noting that her bangles are slipping off due to weight loss and a pale yellow-green tint (pacappu) is spreading across her skin. Despite this suffering and the fact that she is not united with him, she declares that her love remains immense. The poem is set in the kuriñci (mountainous) landscape, where the image of wild rice being sown on the slopes and watered naturally by waterfalls represents how her love grows and is sustained naturally, even in his absence. The key words are 'nekiḻtal' (slipping), 'pacappu' (pallor), and 'kāmam' (love).",
